[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, it's a kind of development on a worldly sense, you could say, but it's not a development of the mind, which is always in regard to feeling.

343
00:34:46,105 --> 00:34:51,930
[SPEAKER_03]: So, all of this feeds into what we discussed earlier in the context of the Middle Way as well.

344
00:34:52,090 --> 00:34:56,934
[SPEAKER_03]: That is basically, like, the right khanthi that leads to the nibbāna.

345
00:34:57,314 --> 00:34:57,895
[SPEAKER_03]: Has to be that.

346
00:34:58,255 --> 00:35:14,949
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, I think we've mentioned that before, that any practice that you're doing, like, whether it's virtue, sense, or strange, or even, like, you know, watching your intentions and purifying your mind from the wholesome states, it would all be determined as right or wrong based on whether you have khanthi.

347
00:35:16,144 --> 00:35:20,946
[SPEAKER_04]: If you're... any of those practices can become wrong, if khanti is lost.

348
00:35:21,347 --> 00:35:35,814
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, you can be doing the same virtue externally, the same sensory change, you can be trying to purify your mind from thoughts of sensuality, but if you're doing it without khanti, basically trying to get rid of the feeling that's there, then it's not really the middle way anymore.

349
00:35:35,974 --> 00:35:37,995
[SPEAKER_04]: That's when you get into self-mortification.

350
00:35:38,175 --> 00:35:38,415
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

351
00:35:52,916 --> 00:35:57,339
[SPEAKER_03]: So, there's this sutta which talks about the handle of an axe, right?

352
00:35:57,359 --> 00:36:15,052
[SPEAKER_03]: The noble disciple practicing the Noble Eightfold Path, he is just reflecting on his Noble Eight View and things we have discussed before, but he is not actually concerned on a daily basis, like, oh, this is how much the handle of this axe... Or the chicken, the hen and the eggs.

353
00:36:15,112 --> 00:36:15,532
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

354
00:36:15,552 --> 00:36:20,236
[SPEAKER_03]: So, he's not concerned with, oh, the handle of this axe has been worn away this much or that much.

355
00:36:20,316 --> 00:36:21,617
[SPEAKER_03]: At some point, he just noticed

356
00:36:22,697 --> 00:36:25,199
[SPEAKER_03]: This whole handle is gone.

357
00:36:25,219 --> 00:36:26,980
[SPEAKER_04]: That's a good one to bring up, because...

358
00:36:28,281 --> 00:36:39,709
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, obviously, that's what it is in the context of the Noble Disciple, but in regards to everyone, like, for as long as, it says, the person is not focused on, oh, I want this result.

359
00:36:40,449 --> 00:36:42,350
[SPEAKER_04]: It's focused on wearing the axe handle down.

360
00:36:42,410 --> 00:36:45,212
[SPEAKER_04]: So, because, why?

361
00:36:45,252 --> 00:36:49,175
[SPEAKER_04]: Because it's not like, it's not saying like, how to put it,

362
00:36:51,310 --> 00:36:56,333
[SPEAKER_04]: It's harmful or it's pointless if you're expecting a result.

363
00:36:56,933 --> 00:37:01,555
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, literally, on account of this, you're not actually developing.

364
00:37:02,076 --> 00:37:04,477
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you're not enduring right there and there.

365
00:37:04,637 --> 00:37:06,178
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, you want a reward out of this?

366
00:37:06,868 --> 00:37:09,669
[SPEAKER_04]: Meaning, you have a problem with this feeling that's now.

367
00:37:09,689 --> 00:37:14,172
[SPEAKER_04]: You have a problem with, well, the pain of not being enlightened, for example.

368
00:37:14,452 --> 00:37:23,016
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you'll be closer to actually developing your mind by enduring the pain of wanting to be enlightened.

369
00:37:23,676 --> 00:37:31,682
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, by just assuming that desire, or that hope, as fine, and then, like, craving, basically, for that.

370
00:37:31,782 --> 00:37:33,443
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, acting out of that, one way or another.

371
00:37:33,604 --> 00:37:34,444
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

372
00:37:34,564 --> 00:37:44,011
[SPEAKER_04]: So, like, enduring ignorance, enduring the recognition that you still are ignorant, basically, that your mind still has defilements, is closer

373
00:37:45,052 --> 00:37:52,998
[SPEAKER_04]: To a brooding of the foments, then just allowing yourself to try to get rid of them in any way you feel is appropriate.

374
00:37:54,539 --> 00:38:08,430
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, again, that would be basically like... unless you understand rightly, and you have the right view, for you, the idea of what the foments are is always mixed with what feeding is.

375
00:38:09,390 --> 00:38:12,273
[SPEAKER_04]: So, like, a person would be trying to get rid of the foments, but

376
00:38:13,325 --> 00:38:17,907
[SPEAKER_04]: More often than not, they will be just trying to get rid of feelings, because they don't see that separation.

377
00:38:17,947 --> 00:38:27,412
[SPEAKER_04]: That separation between what is a pleasant feeling and what is sensuality, what is unpleasant feeling and what is aversion, that can only be recognized with the right view.

378
00:38:28,073 --> 00:38:30,294
[SPEAKER_04]: That's where the... that's where, um...

379
00:38:30,994 --> 00:38:39,117
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's why it says, Majjhima Nikaya 9, that only a knower disciple understands wholesome is wholesome and unwholesome is unwholesome, because that's when you can separate.

380
00:38:39,657 --> 00:38:46,460
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, so this is the actual part that is in my control, which is the defilement, and this is the part that arises on its own.

381
00:38:47,921 --> 00:39:01,449
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's also like seeing the sign of the mind, recognizing the extent to which things are just given, which is feelings, and the extent to which you're basically either enduring them or not, which is wholesome or unwholesome.

382
00:39:03,951 --> 00:39:09,414
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, and this notion of not being fully completely enlightened yet, and the craving involved in that,

383
00:39:10,600 --> 00:39:12,040
[SPEAKER_03]: That is paired with ill-will.

384
00:39:12,161 --> 00:39:16,982
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, there can be a sense of aggression or ill-will in one's attitude towards oneself.

385
00:39:17,242 --> 00:39:19,222
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, why do I not have that?

386
00:39:19,782 --> 00:39:37,927
[SPEAKER_03]: But, as you just said, like, by enduring that or having patience on the right level, even in relation to that, that is how one actually can give up that ill-will, which is precisely the, let's say, gentleness or meekness, which is often paired with a khanti.

387
00:39:38,743 --> 00:39:47,571
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's why there's no justification ever to say, this feeling I don't need to endure, these other ones I do.

388
00:39:47,751 --> 00:39:54,136
[SPEAKER_04]: Because that's where the Buddha says, oh, any feeling, coarse or fine, fierce, pure,

389
00:39:57,118 --> 00:40:01,280
[SPEAKER_04]: All feelings should be seen in the same way.

390
00:40:01,980 --> 00:40:13,906
[SPEAKER_04]: So, like, you shouldn't be making exceptions for, okay, the feeling of, like, for example, the feeling I get when I have a sensual thought, it's an unpleasant feeling.

391
00:40:14,966 --> 00:40:21,890
[SPEAKER_04]: And I assume that because the Buddha says you have to get rid of central thoughts, I'm justified of getting rid of this feeling.

392
00:40:22,490 --> 00:40:26,592
[SPEAKER_04]: You have to get rid of the central thought, not get rid of the feeling.

393
00:40:26,752 --> 00:40:29,974
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's why, like, a person needs to recognize...

394
00:40:30,734 --> 00:40:33,876
[SPEAKER_04]: That there is a distinction there to be reckoned, to be seen.

395
00:40:35,618 --> 00:40:42,243
[SPEAKER_04]: This pleasure of any unwholesome state that you might be experiencing is not the same as the unwholesome state itself.

396
00:40:42,883 --> 00:40:48,828
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you just assume and allow yourself to get rid of it, like, on impulse, basically,

397
00:40:49,468 --> 00:40:53,712
[SPEAKER_04]: You'll basically just be throwing the baby out with the bath water, as the saying goes.

398
00:40:54,613 --> 00:41:01,019
[SPEAKER_04]: So, a person needs to learn, like, how to endure things without giving in to them at the same time.

399
00:41:01,560 --> 00:41:05,303
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, for most people, like, they say, oh, no, I don't have to get rid of it.

400
00:41:05,363 --> 00:41:06,304
[SPEAKER_04]: I just have to endure it.

401
00:41:06,825 --> 00:41:10,748
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, that means, oh, I stay with the sensual thought now, but I'm kind of delighting in it still.

402
00:41:10,909 --> 00:41:11,930
[SPEAKER_04]: It's still kind of...

403
00:41:14,171 --> 00:41:18,495
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's like always finding that right balance.

404
00:41:18,695 --> 00:41:20,376
[SPEAKER_04]: How do I not welcome things?

405
00:41:20,596 --> 00:41:25,220
[SPEAKER_04]: How do I remain dispassionate towards things, but without trying to get rid of them?

406
00:41:28,870 --> 00:41:38,356
[SPEAKER_04]: And dispassion as well can often be thought of as like, oh, I need to be kind of not feeling things anymore, so as to be dispassionate.

407
00:41:38,396 --> 00:41:42,519
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, I just... basically confused with like apathy.

408
00:41:42,779 --> 00:41:43,020
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

409
00:41:43,040 --> 00:41:46,762
[SPEAKER_03]: So, because the dispassion is precisely in relation to feeling.

410
00:41:46,862 --> 00:41:47,663
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, exactly.

411
00:41:47,703 --> 00:41:50,404
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's not like dispassion is not lack of feeling.

412
00:41:50,985 --> 00:41:55,188
[SPEAKER_04]: Dispassion is like not needing to act out no matter what feeling is there.

413
00:41:55,368 --> 00:41:55,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

414
00:41:57,177 --> 00:42:05,123
[SPEAKER_04]: Because apathy is just like... again, it's like no stimulus, therefore no response.

415
00:42:06,484 --> 00:42:08,986
[SPEAKER_04]: But that's not... that's not training.

416
00:42:09,006 --> 00:42:10,447
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, that's overshooting the market.

417
00:42:10,487 --> 00:42:10,767
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

418
00:42:11,348 --> 00:42:17,793
[SPEAKER_04]: Like if you say, I won because there's no threat anymore.

419
00:42:19,685 --> 00:42:21,667
[SPEAKER_04]: Or, I'm safe because there's no threat anymore.

420
00:42:21,727 --> 00:42:23,989
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's a circumstantial safety.

421
00:42:24,369 --> 00:42:28,052
[SPEAKER_04]: You have to be safe when there is a threat, essentially.

422
00:42:28,272 --> 00:42:30,193
[SPEAKER_04]: And every feeling, basically, is a threat.

423
00:42:30,654 --> 00:42:30,874
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

